# ## 10. Self-check
#
# 1. A tool's pin is unchanged after a month of daily checks. What does that tell you?
#    - (a) The description is safe, because a safe tool never needs to change, and a tool nobody edits cannot have been poisoned.
#    - (b) The definition is byte-for-byte what you approved; whether it was safe is a separate question.
#    - (c) The server's owner is who the registry says it is, because the pin was taken from the registry.
#    - (d) The scanner, run today with whatever rules it has now, would certainly still find nothing in it.
#
# 2. Why does `scan_tool` append the decoded Tag message to the description before it normalises?
#    - (a) So that a hidden sentence is checked by the same rules as a visible one.
#    - (b) So that the text is shorter and the later rules have less to read.
#    - (c) Because Tag characters are not part of Unicode and cannot be searched for directly.
#    - (d) So that the pin changes whenever the hidden message changes.
#
# 3. A registry says a server's namespace is verified. What has it told you?
#    - (a) The server's tool descriptions contain no instructions, because the registry checks them before it lists the server.
#    - (b) The tools were reviewed by the registry's maintainers before the namespace was verified.
#    - (c) The publisher controls the domain or account the namespace names; what the descriptions say is a separate question.
#    - (d) The server cannot change its tools after publishing, because the registry keeps a copy of each list and refuses edits.
#
# 4. A scanner scores far higher on attacks its author wrote than on paraphrases written afterwards. The best reading is:
#    - (a) The paraphrased set is unfair, so the score on the author's own attacks is the real one and the paraphrases can be ignored.
#    - (b) The author's set was written with the rules in mind, so its recall flatters the scanner; phrase lists are brittle.
#    - (c) Recall does not matter when precision is high, because a scanner that rarely cries wolf is a safe one.
#    - (d) The scanner should be given longer phrase lists until the second number reaches the first, and then it is safe.
#
# Answers, with reasoning, come with this lesson's worked solution when you enrol on Synapsa.

